// MUTUAL RECURSION MAINTAINED ACROSS EPOCHS — TWO RELS IN ONE SCC, CHECKED AT
// EVERY EPOCH ON CYCLIC DATA, AND WITH AN ALTERNATION-DEPTH SENSOR.
//
// P3b admitted exactly ONE shape: a single-member recursive SCC. `nmem != 1`
// refused everything else, and the ground it named was "no fixture in this slice
// runs a mutually recursive rel across epochs". This file is that fixture, and
// P3c is the widening it admits.
//
//     rel odd (a,b)  :- es(a,b)
//     rel odd (a,b)  :- even(a,c), es(c,b)
//     rel even(a,b)  :- odd(a,c),  es(c,b)
//
// `odd` and `even` are ONE SCC of two members: odd's second body scans even, and
// even's only body scans odd. `odd(a,b)` = there is a path a→b of ODD length;
// `even(a,b)` = one of EVEN length ≥ 2.
//
// ── WHAT CHANGED IN THE EMITTER, so a reader knows what is under test ──────
//  · the handle grows ONE `__tot_<m>` field PER SCC MEMBER (`__edb` stays one —
//    every member reads the same single slice parameter);
//  · `<q>_epoch` seeds N totals into locals, passes N `&mut Vec` to the driver,
//    and commits all N AFTER it returns (the transactional property is now N
//    writes that must land together);
//  · ONLY the selected member is FOLDED into the group state — the co-member is
//    maintained because the fixpoint needs it, and folded by nothing because
//    nothing asked about it. That is why this file runs TWO queries.
//
// ── THE ORDER HAZARD THIS FILE EXISTS TO CATCH, STATED PLAINLY ─────────────
// ⚠⚠ BOTH MEMBERS HAVE ROW TYPE `(i64, i64)`. The driver takes the totals as
// POSITIONAL out-params in registry order, so binding them in the wrong order
// TYPECHECKS and answers with the two relations EXCHANGED. No compiler
// diagnostic exists for it; only an assertion that can tell odd from even does.
// `oddq` and `evenq` disagree at E1 (odd has one group, even has none), at E2
// (2 groups vs 1) and across the whole chain block (12 groups vs 11, and key 2
// answers 6 vs 5) — so a swap is caught, and caught at depth as well as early.
//
// ── THE CYCLIC DATA, AND WHY A DAG COULD NOT SEE IT ────────────────────────
// Seven epochs, ONE edge each:
//
//   E1 (1,2)   E2 (2,3)   E3 (3,1) — closes the 3-cycle
//   E4 (2,2) — a SELF-LOOP already in the closure
//   E5 (1,4)   E6 (3,4) — already reachable   E7 (1,2) — a DUPLICATE
//
// A 3-cycle is ODD, so once E3 closes it BOTH parities become reachable between
// every pair of {1,2,3} and the two relations coincide from E3 on. That is a
// property worth having in the table (it is where mutual recursion actually
// saturates) and it is precisely why it cannot be the depth sensor: E1 and E2
// are the only cyclic-block epochs where odd and even differ.
//
// ── THE ANSWERS, DERIVED BY HAND AND THEN CHECKED AGAINST AN INDEPENDENT
// ── ENUMERATION BEFORE ANYTHING WAS COMPILED ───────────────────────────────
// `oddq` is `from odd p group by p.a aggregate c = count()`, so the table is
// key → |{ b : odd(key,b) }|; `evenq` the same over `even`. `-1` = absent.
//
//        odd:  1   2   3          even: 1   2   3
//   E1        1  -1  -1                -1  -1  -1
//   E2        1   1  -1                 1  -1  -1
//   E3        3   3   3                 3   3   3     ← the cycle closes
//   E4        3   3   3                 3   3   3     ← UNCHANGED
//   E5        4   4   4                 4   4   4
//   E6        4   4   4                 4   4   4     ← UNCHANGED
//   E7        4   4   4                 4   4   4     ← UNCHANGED (a rel is a SET)
//
// ── THREE WITNESSES, BECAUSE AN ORACLE THAT SHARES THE ALGORITHM IS NOT ONE ─
//  (i)   the table above;
//  (ii)  `naive_alt` — a from-scratch ALTERNATING closure recomputed over the
//        accumulated edge list at every epoch: repeat `O := E ∪ (V∘E)`,
//        `V := O∘E` until neither grows, dedup by linear `==`. No semi-naive
//        rewriting, no delta, no shadow set, no SCC, no stratification. It
//        shares no code with the emitter and none with the interpreter.
//  (iii) the batch fns `oddq()`/`evenq()` over the same accumulated edges — a
//        CONTROL, NOT AN ORACLE. They go through the SAME `emit_scc_fn`, so
//        they would agree with a wrong semi-naive rewriting. They separate "the
//        driver is wrong" from "the cross-epoch entry is wrong", and certify
//        neither. This arc has twice been burned by three sides running one
//        wrong fold; (ii) is the one that is allowed to say a number is right.
//
// ── THE CONTROLS THAT WERE RUN AGAINST THIS FILE, WITH THEIR CODES ────────
// Each is an edit to the emitter, archives dropped, whole stdlib rebuilt.
//   · the P3b refusal restored (`prm.rel_n != 1`)      → does not COMPILE
//                                                        (`unknown type 'OddqIncr'`)
//   · member totals bound to the driver in reverse     → exit 15  (the artifact block)
//   · the co-member folded instead of the selected one → exit 25  (E1)
//   · the `==` dedup gate in front of `__edb` deleted  → ABORT at E7 (the |__edb| bound)
//   · the fixpoint stopped one round early             → exit 90  (E3 — see the note
//                                                        at the depth block: this one
//                                                        MASKS the depth axis)
//   · the fixpoint capped at 6 rounds                  → exit 255 (the depth cell,
//                                                        with E1-E7 green)
//   · retraction through recursion re-enabled          → the two `fail/` doors compile
// The one edit NOTHING here catches — the `_i` driver emitted once per admitted
// MEMBER rather than once per SCC — is why `incr_scc_driver_gate.sh` exists.
//
// exit 0 = all three witnesses agree with the table at all seven epochs, for
// BOTH members, the metamorphic split and the saturated re-feed hold, and the
// alternation-depth block answers 6.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.std.wql.el;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.mem.string;
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct E { pub a: i64, pub b: i64 }

// ⚠ TWO QUERIES OVER THE SAME PAIR, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Each gets its own
// handle with its own group state; each maintains BOTH totals; each folds only
// its own member. A handle that folded the co-member, or bound the totals in the
// wrong order, answers the other query's table.
pub deem oddq(es: &[E]) {
    rel odd(a: i64, b: i64) {
        from es e select (e.a, e.b);
        from even v join es e on v.b == e.a select (v.a, e.b);
    }
    rel even(a: i64, b: i64) {
        from odd o join es e on o.b == e.a select (o.a, e.b);
    }
    from odd p group by p.a aggregate c = count() select (key, c)
}

pub deem evenq(es: &[E]) {
    rel odd(a: i64, b: i64) {
        from es e select (e.a, e.b);
        from even v join es e on v.b == e.a select (v.a, e.b);
    }
    rel even(a: i64, b: i64) {
        from odd o join es e on o.b == e.a select (o.a, e.b);
    }
    from even p group by p.a aggregate c = count() select (key, c)
}

// ── WITNESS (ii): the from-scratch ALTERNATING closure ─────────────────────
fn pair_uniq(p: &mut Vec<(i64, i64)>, a: i64, b: i64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < p.len() {
        let c: (i64, i64) = p.get(i);
        if c.0 == a && c.1 == b { return false; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    p.push((a, b));
    return true;
}

// `want_odd` selects WHICH of the two relations comes back. Both are computed
// either way — the alternation is the whole mechanism and it cannot be run for
// one side alone, which is itself the fact this fixture is about.
fn naive_alt(es: &[E], want_odd: bool) -> Vec<(i64, i64)> {
    let mut o: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut v: Vec<(i64, i64)> = vec_new::<(i64, i64)>();
    let mut grew: bool = true;
    while grew {
        grew = false;
        // odd :- es
        let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
        while i < es.len() {
            if pair_uniq(&mut o, es[i].a, es[i].b) { grew = true; }
            i = i + 1i64;
        }
        // odd :- even ∘ es
        let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
        while k < v.len() {
            let pr: (i64, i64) = v.get(k);
            let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
            while j < es.len() {
                if pr.1 == es[j].a {
                    if pair_uniq(&mut o, pr.0, es[j].b) { grew = true; }
                }
                j = j + 1i64;
            }
            k = k + 1i64;
        }
        // even :- odd ∘ es
        let mut k2: i64 = 0i64;
        while k2 < o.len() {
            let pr2: (i64, i64) = o.get(k2);
            let mut j2: i64 = 0i64;
            while j2 < es.len() {
                if pr2.1 == es[j2].a {
                    if pair_uniq(&mut v, pr2.0, es[j2].b) { grew = true; }
                }
                j2 = j2 + 1i64;
            }
            k2 = k2 + 1i64;
        }
    }
    if want_odd { return o; }
    return v;
}

fn naive_count(es: &[E], want_odd: bool, key: i64) -> i64 {
    let p: Vec<(i64, i64)> = naive_alt(es, want_odd);
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < p.len() {
        if p.get(i).0 == key { n = n + 1i64; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    if n == 0i64 { return -1i64; }
    return n;
}

// ⚠ LOOKED UP BY KEY, NEVER BY POSITION — group order is first-occurrence order
// and asserting by index would hide a wrong count behind a right order. `-1` =
// the group is ABSENT, a distinct and assertable answer from "present with 0".
fn cnt_of(out: &Vec<(i64, i64)>, key: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < out.len() {
        let r: (i64, i64) = out.get(i);
        if r.0 == key { return r.1; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return -1i64;
}

fn edge(a: i64, b: i64) -> Vec<E> {
    let mut v: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    v.push(E { a: a, b: b });
    return v;
}

// One epoch on BOTH handles. Returns 0 on agreement, else a code naming WHICH
// witness, WHICH member and WHICH key disagreed; the caller adds the epoch.
fn step(ho: &mut OddqIncr, he: &mut EvenqIncr, acc: &mut Vec<E>, d: &Vec<E>,
        o1: i64, o2: i64, o3: i64, v1: i64, v2: i64, v3: i64) -> i32 {
    match oddq_epoch(ho, d.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 1i32; }
    }
    match evenq_epoch(he, d.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 2i32; }
    }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < d.len() { acc.push(d.get(i)); i = i + 1i64; }

    let oo: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match oddq_snapshot(ho) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 3i32; }
    };
    let ve: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match evenq_snapshot(he) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 4i32; }
    };
    // (i) THE HAND TABLE, both members, every key.
    if cnt_of(&oo, 1i64) != o1 { return 5i32; }
    if cnt_of(&oo, 2i64) != o2 { return 6i32; }
    if cnt_of(&oo, 3i64) != o3 { return 7i32; }
    if cnt_of(&ve, 1i64) != v1 { return 8i32; }
    if cnt_of(&ve, 2i64) != v2 { return 9i32; }
    if cnt_of(&ve, 3i64) != v3 { return 10i32; }
    // ⚠ THE GROUP POPULATION, BY COUNT — without it a handle that invented a
    // fourth group nobody asked about passes every lookup above. DERIVED from
    // the table (`-1` means absent) rather than passed in.
    let mut wo: i64 = 0i64;
    if o1 >= 0i64 { wo = wo + 1i64; }
    if o2 >= 0i64 { wo = wo + 1i64; }
    if o3 >= 0i64 { wo = wo + 1i64; }
    if oo.len() != wo { return 11i32; }
    let mut wv: i64 = 0i64;
    if v1 >= 0i64 { wv = wv + 1i64; }
    if v2 >= 0i64 { wv = wv + 1i64; }
    if v3 >= 0i64 { wv = wv + 1i64; }
    if ve.len() != wv { return 12i32; }

    // (ii) THE INDEPENDENT ALTERNATING CLOSURE over the accumulated input.
    if naive_count(acc.as_slice(), true,  1i64) != o1 { return 13i32; }
    if naive_count(acc.as_slice(), true,  2i64) != o2 { return 14i32; }
    if naive_count(acc.as_slice(), true,  3i64) != o3 { return 15i32; }
    if naive_count(acc.as_slice(), false, 1i64) != v1 { return 16i32; }
    if naive_count(acc.as_slice(), false, 2i64) != v2 { return 17i32; }
    if naive_count(acc.as_slice(), false, 3i64) != v3 { return 18i32; }

    // (iii) THE BATCH CONTROL — same emitter; agreement says the cross-epoch
    // entry did not diverge from the from-scratch one, and nothing about
    // whether either is right.
    let bo: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match oddq(acc.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 19i32; }
    };
    let bv: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match evenq(acc.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 20i32; }
    };
    if bo.len() != oo.len() { return 21i32; }
    if bv.len() != ve.len() { return 22i32; }
    if cnt_of(&bo, 1i64) != o1 { return 23i32; }
    if cnt_of(&bo, 2i64) != o2 { return 24i32; }
    if cnt_of(&bo, 3i64) != o3 { return 25i32; }
    if cnt_of(&bv, 1i64) != v1 { return 26i32; }
    if cnt_of(&bv, 2i64) != v2 { return 27i32; }
    if cnt_of(&bv, 3i64) != v3 { return 28i32; }
    return 0i32;
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut ho: OddqIncr  = oddq_incremental();
    let mut he: EvenqIncr = evenq_incremental();
    let mut acc: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();

    // ══ THE ARTIFACT ITSELF, ASSERTED BEFORE ANY ANSWER IS ═════════════════
    //
    // ⚠ EVERYTHING BELOW THIS BLOCK IS AN ANSWER, AND AN ANSWER CAN BE RIGHT
    // FOR THE WRONG REASON. What P3c actually changed is the SHAPE of the
    // handle — one `__tot_<m>` per SCC member, `__edb` still one — and the
    // ORDER those totals bind to the driver's positional out-params. These four
    // lines name the fields, so the widening is pinned by a reference that does
    // not compile if the field is missing, and by a COUNT that differs between
    // the two members so a swapped binding cannot satisfy both.
    //
    // After the two edges (1,2) and (2,3): odd = {(1,2),(2,3)} — TWO rows —
    // and even = {(1,3)} — ONE. Swap the totals and this block reads 1 and 2.
    let mut pv: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    pv.push(E { a: 1i64, b: 2i64 });
    pv.push(E { a: 2i64, b: 3i64 });
    let mut hp: OddqIncr = oddq_incremental();
    if hp.__tot_odd.len()  != 0i64 { return 11i32; }
    if hp.__tot_even.len() != 0i64 { return 12i32; }
    match oddq_epoch(&mut hp, pv.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 13i32; }
    }
    if hp.__edb.len()      != 2i64 { return 14i32; }
    if hp.__tot_odd.len()  != 2i64 { return 15i32; }
    if hp.__tot_even.len() != 1i64 { return 16i32; }

    // E1 — (1,2). odd gains one row; even is still EMPTY, which is the one
    // cheap place a swapped total binding shows up as an absent group.
    let r1: i32 = step(&mut ho, &mut he, &mut acc, &edge(1i64, 2i64),
                       1i64, -1i64, -1i64, -1i64, -1i64, -1i64);
    if r1 != 0i32 { return 20i32 + r1; }
    // E2 — (2,3). THE ALTERNATION STARTS: even(1,3) comes from odd(1,2) joined
    // with a NEW edge, and odd(2,3) from the base body. The two members now
    // carry DIFFERENT group populations (2 vs 1).
    let r2: i32 = step(&mut ho, &mut he, &mut acc, &edge(2i64, 3i64),
                       1i64, 1i64, -1i64, 1i64, -1i64, -1i64);
    if r2 != 0i32 { return 50i32 + r2; }
    // E3 — (3,1) CLOSES AN ODD CYCLE, so both parities reach everywhere.
    let r3: i32 = step(&mut ho, &mut he, &mut acc, &edge(3i64, 1i64),
                       3i64, 3i64, 3i64, 3i64, 3i64, 3i64);
    if r3 != 0i32 { return 80i32 + r3; }
    // E4 — (2,2) a SELF-LOOP already derivable. Nothing may move on either side.
    let r4: i32 = step(&mut ho, &mut he, &mut acc, &edge(2i64, 2i64),
                       3i64, 3i64, 3i64, 3i64, 3i64, 3i64);
    if r4 != 0i32 { return 110i32 + r4; }
    // E5 — (1,4). Node 4 becomes reachable at BOTH parities from all three.
    let r5: i32 = step(&mut ho, &mut he, &mut acc, &edge(1i64, 4i64),
                       4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64);
    if r5 != 0i32 { return 140i32 + r5; }
    // E6 — (3,4), a SECOND route to an already-reached node. Nothing may move.
    let r6: i32 = step(&mut ho, &mut he, &mut acc, &edge(3i64, 4i64),
                       4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64);
    if r6 != 0i32 { return 170i32 + r6; }
    // E7 — (1,2) A DUPLICATE EDGE. A rel is a SET; nothing may move. ⚠ THIS IS
    // ALSO THE EPOCH THAT EXERCISES THE `|__edb|` BOUND: the emitted epoch walks
    // the rows it merged this epoch against every row that preceded them and
    // PANICS if the dedup gate let a repeat through. Defeating that gate reds
    // HERE — measured — where before this bound existed it moved no answer
    // anywhere in the tree.
    let r7: i32 = step(&mut ho, &mut he, &mut acc, &edge(1i64, 2i64),
                       4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64, 4i64);
    if r7 != 0i32 { return 200i32 + r7; }

    // ── THE METAMORPHIC LAW. For any SPLIT of one edge multiset into epochs,
    // the snapshot equals the batch over the concatenation. Above it is seven
    // singletons; here it is ONE epoch carrying all seven — the shape in which
    // per-epoch and cross-epoch dedup are the SAME code path, so a handle that
    // deduped only within a call passes this and fails above, and one that
    // deduped only across calls does the reverse.
    let mut ho2: OddqIncr = oddq_incremental();
    match oddq_epoch(&mut ho2, acc.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 230i32; }
    }
    let o2: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match oddq_snapshot(&ho2) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 231i32; }
    };
    if o2.len() != 3i64          { return 232i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o2, 1i64) != 4i64 { return 233i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o2, 2i64) != 4i64 { return 234i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o2, 3i64) != 4i64 { return 235i32; }

    // ── A SATURATED HANDLE RE-FED ITS OWN INPUT MUST NOT MOVE. Every fact is
    // already present, so BOTH totals gain nothing and the fold sees an EMPTY
    // suffix. A `+1` over the whole total rather than over the new rows doubles
    // every count here; so does a weight crossing outbound.
    match oddq_epoch(&mut ho2, acc.as_slice()) {
        Result::Ok(n) => { if n != 0i64 { return 236i32; } }
        Result::Err(_e) => { return 237i32; }
    }
    let o3: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match oddq_snapshot(&ho2) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 238i32; }
    };
    if o3.len() != 3i64          { return 239i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o3, 1i64) != 4i64 { return 240i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o3, 2i64) != 4i64 { return 241i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o3, 3i64) != 4i64 { return 242i32; }

    // ══ THE ALTERNATION-DEPTH SENSOR ═══════════════════════════════════════
    //
    // ⚠ EVERY EPOCH ABOVE IS DECIDED SHALLOW. That is the lesson E8 taught the
    // single-member fixture and it applies verbatim here: four independent
    // injected defects were each caught on a two-edge prefix, so the file was
    // blind to an engine that is RIGHT at depth 2 and WRONG at depth k. For
    // MUTUAL recursion the depth axis is not edge count — it is the number of
    // ALTERNATIONS between the two members, and nothing in the cyclic block
    // above needs more than one.
    //
    // The chain 1→2→…→13 (12 edges). odd(1,12) has length 11, so its derivation
    // is odd(1,2) → even(1,3) → odd(1,4) → … → odd(1,12): ELEVEN rule
    // applications, TEN of them crossing from one member to the other. The chain
    // is fed FROM THE TAIL — (12,13) first, (1,2) last — so every epoch but the
    // last extends a chain node 1 cannot yet see, and the final single edge must
    // cascade eleven alternating rounds IN ONE EPOCH.
    //
    // Closed form on a simple chain: odd = {(i,j) : j−i odd}, even = {(i,j) :
    // j−i even, j>i}. Key `a` answers ⌈(13−a)/2⌉ in odd and ⌊(13−a)/2⌋ in even,
    // so odd has 12 groups (1..12) and even 11 (1..11) — and key 2 answers 6 in
    // odd against 5 in even, which is the swap check at depth.
    //
    // ⚠ ASSERTED ONLY AT THE LAST PREFIX, deliberately. Checking every prefix
    // would let an early divergence fire first and mask the depth behaviour,
    // which is exactly how the rest of this file reports its shallowest epoch
    // for everything.
    let mut ho4: OddqIncr  = oddq_incremental();
    let mut he4: EvenqIncr = evenq_incremental();
    let mut src: i64 = 12i64;
    while src >= 1i64 {
        match oddq_epoch(&mut ho4, edge(src, src + 1i64).as_slice()) {
            Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 250i32; }
        }
        match evenq_epoch(&mut he4, edge(src, src + 1i64).as_slice()) {
            Result::Ok(_n) => { } Result::Err(_e) => { return 251i32; }
        }
        src = src - 1i64;
    }
    let o4: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match oddq_snapshot(&ho4) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 252i32; }
    };
    let v4: Vec<(i64, i64)> = match evenq_snapshot(&he4) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 253i32; }
    };
    if o4.len() != 12i64           { return 254i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o4, 1i64) != 6i64   { return 255i32; }   // ← the depth cell: (1,12) is 11 rounds deep
    if cnt_of(&o4, 2i64) != 6i64   { return 256i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o4, 7i64) != 3i64   { return 257i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o4, 12i64) != 1i64  { return 258i32; }
    if cnt_of(&o4, 13i64) != -1i64 { return 259i32; }
    // The co-member at the same depth. `even` is MAINTAINED by `oddq`'s handle
    // and never folded there; here its own handle folds it, and its shape
    // differs from odd's — 11 groups, key 2 = 5 — so the two cannot be confused.
    if v4.len() != 11i64           { return 260i32; }
    if cnt_of(&v4, 1i64) != 6i64   { return 261i32; }
    if cnt_of(&v4, 2i64) != 5i64   { return 262i32; }
    if cnt_of(&v4, 11i64) != 1i64  { return 263i32; }
    if cnt_of(&v4, 12i64) != -1i64 { return 264i32; }
    // And the INDEPENDENT witness at the deepest prefix, because everything
    // above this line is the hand table and the batch control.
    let mut ch: Vec<E> = vec_new::<E>();
    let mut cs: i64 = 1i64;
    while cs <= 12i64 { ch.push(E { a: cs, b: cs + 1i64 }); cs = cs + 1i64; }
    if naive_count(ch.as_slice(), true,  1i64) != 6i64 { return 265i32; }
    if naive_count(ch.as_slice(), true,  2i64) != 6i64 { return 266i32; }
    if naive_count(ch.as_slice(), false, 2i64) != 5i64 { return 267i32; }
    // ⚠⚠ PROVEN TO CATCH A CLASS NOTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE CATCHES, AND THE
    // FIRST ATTEMPT AT PROVING IT FAILED — both are recorded, because the
    // failed one is the more useful.
    //
    // CONTROL A (2026-08-07), "stop the fixpoint one round early": the exit
    // condition `__dl_<m>.len() == 0i64` weakened to `<= 1i64`, archives
    // dropped, full stdlib rebuild. Result: **exit 90** — E3, in the cyclic
    // block, five hundred lines above here. The depth block NEVER SPOKE. That
    // is not a success for this sensor: an odd 3-cycle saturates through both
    // members in a handful of rounds, so an engine that drops the last round is
    // already wrong at E3 and the shallow epoch reports first. (The
    // single-member fixture reds at 173 under the same edit, so the defect is
    // real in both.)
    //
    // CONTROL B, the one this block exists for: CAP the fixpoint at 6 rounds
    // (`if __capk > 6i64 { break; }` inside the driver's loop) — an engine that
    // is exactly right wherever saturation is shallow and wrong exactly where
    // the answer needs depth. Result: **exit 255**, the depth cell above.
    // E1-E7 GREEN. The metamorphic split GREEN. The saturated re-feed GREEN.
    // Every one of the twelve alternation epochs before the last GREEN, because
    // nothing is asserted at them. The co-member block, the population counts
    // and the independent witness all sit behind that same 255.
    // A defect right at alternation depth 3 and wrong at depth 11 has a sensor.
    return 0i32;
}
